            },
            "ref_text": "- can be sped up\n- We obtain similar accuracy to SGD without fine-tuning (not sure what this refers to).",
            "test_text": "- CLAR: The mathematical notation for the component function $c_l$ and its parametrization is inconsistent and potentially confusing regarding dimensions, specifically whether $c_l$ represents the layer as a whole or a single component. Evidence anchors: SRC-003.\n\n- Suggested revision for CLAR opinion 2: Clarify that $c_{li}$ is the $i$-th component of layer $l$, and define the input/output dimensions of a single component $c_{li}$ explicitly to avoid confusion with the layer-level function $f_l$.\n\n- CLAR: The definition of the gradient matrix $G_l$ and the subsequent orthogonalization step could be clearer regarding how the gradients are stacked, specifically that the columns are flattened gradient vectors. Evidence anchors: SRC-003.\n\n- Suggested revision for CLAR opinion 5: Explicitly state that the columns of $G_l$ are the flattened gradient vectors for each component $c_{li}$, and that the SVD is performed on this $P_l \\times N_l$ matrix."
          },
          "ORIG-MTH": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": "X",
              "alignment": 4,
              "average": 4.25,
              "claim_extraction": "normal",
              "clarity": 5,
              "completeness": 4,
              "grounding_specificity": 4,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"normal\",\n  \"actionability\": \"X\",\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": \"X\",\n  \"alignment\": 4,\n  \"completeness\": 4,\n  \"clarity\": 5\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": "X"
            },
            "ref_text": "Strengths\n* Orthogonalising gradients, rather than weights, seems to be a novel idea.\nWeaknesses",
            "test_text": "- ORIG: The paper introduces a novel optimization algorithm, Orthogonal-SGDM, which applies SVD-based orthogonalization to the gradient matrix of layer components, representing a distinct algorithmic modification to standard first-order optimizers. Evidence anchors: SRC-004."
          },
          "QUAL-CMP": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": 4,
              "alignment": 4,
              "average": 3.6666666666666665,
              "claim_extraction": "claim",
              "clarity": 4,
              "completeness": 3,
              "grounding_specificity": 4,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"claim\",\n  \"actionability\": 4,\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": 3,\n  \"alignment\": 4,\n  \"completeness\": 3,\n  \"clarity\": 4\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": 3
            },
            "ref_text": "I am recommending a reject.\nNevertheless, the proposed idea seems interesting and I strongly urge the authors to study neural network optimization papers that look into the convergence speed of deep networks. Here, usually, experiments are not (mainly) done on very large networks / datasets and so the authors might have an easier time investigating the proposed method under limited compute (since they mention this limitation).\nSee for example the experiments conducted in https://arxiv.org/pdf/1503.05671.pdf.\n\n- (1) ResNet34 baseline should get something around 73% on ImageNet.\nSimilarly I found CIFAR accuracies to be very low. ResNet-20 with batch normalization should get 92-93% on Cifar10. I recommend authors to use strong baselines, otherwise it is very difficult to convince the reader whether this method is useful in practice.\n\n- (3) Would using ortogonal regularization and orthogonal initilization fix the problem? As explained briefly in the summary, orthogonality is widely studied in neural networks. Authors need to discuss and compare their methods against previous methods.\n\n## After Rebuttal\nI read authors response and most of the limitations (1,3,5) are still a concern, thus I keep my score and encourage authors to address them in the future revisions.\nNO.\n\n* Discussion and empirical comparison to show how this is different to other orthogonalisation and projection-based optimisation/regularisation approaches is needed.\n\n* Perhaps I should have been more clear: I would like to see discussion about *why* orthogonalising gradients will have a different effect to orthogonalising weights.\nNO.",
            "test_text": "- QUAL: The experimental evaluation lacks comparisons against modern state-of-the-art optimizers (e.g., Adam, AdamW, LAMB) and recent baselines, limiting the generalizability of the claims regarding speedup and robustness. Evidence anchors: SRC-005, SRC-007.\n\n- Suggested revision for QUAL opinion 2: Include comparisons with Adam/AdamW and other recent optimizers to demonstrate the relative advantage of Orthogonal-SGDM in the current landscape.\n\n- QUAL: The ImageNet results are presented as single-run accuracies without standard error or variance, and the absolute performance (67.5%) is significantly below standard ResNet-34 benchmarks, making the speedup claim less convincing without a clear epoch-matched comparison. Evidence anchors: SRC-008.\n\n- QUAL: The paper does not sufficiently compare against other methods that address feature redundancy or diversity (e.g., orthogonal weight constraints, spectral normalization, or other diversity-promoting regularizers), making it difficult to isolate the specific benefit of gradient orthogonalization. Evidence anchors: SRC-002.\n\n- Suggested revision for QUAL opinion 6: Discuss and compare with prior work on orthogonal weight constraints or other diversity-promoting techniques to contextualize the contribution."
          },
          "QUAL-EXP": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": 4,
              "alignment": 4,
              "average": 3.5,
              "claim_extraction": "claim",
              "clarity": 4,
              "completeness": 2,
              "grounding_specificity": 4,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"claim\",\n  \"actionability\": 4,\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": 3,\n  \"alignment\": 4,\n  \"completeness\": 2,\n  \"clarity\": 4\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": 3
            },
            "ref_text": "The conducted experiments do not support the author's main claim of improved training speed. This should be measured on the training loss.\nTherefore all presented Figures can only support claims dealing with generalization error - is there a reason for not showing the training loss curves? I find this irritating.\n\nI am recommending a reject.\nNevertheless, the proposed idea seems interesting and I strongly urge the authors to study neural network optimization papers that look into the convergence speed of deep networks. Here, usually, experiments are not (mainly) done on very large networks / datasets and so the authors might have an easier time investigating the proposed method under limited compute (since they mention this limitation).\nSee for example the experiments conducted in https://arxiv.org/pdf/1503.05671.pdf.\n\n* The performance comparisons are lacking. Picking one set of hyperparameters is not a sufficient procedure for comparing two algorithms. It could be the case that the particular set of hyperparameters chosen are well-suited to Orthogonal-SGDM, but maybe there exists another set of hyperparameters that allows SGDM to achieve superior performance. Given the unusually low performance of the different ResNet models considered in the experiments, this seems like a very real possibility.\n\nThanks for the comments and improvements.\nAlthough validation set accuracy is what many people are interested in, training set accuracy should be the focus of this paper and is a very important problem on its own.\n\n- (1) ResNet34 baseline should get something around 73% on ImageNet.\nSimilarly I found CIFAR accuracies to be very low. ResNet-20 with batch normalization should get 92-93% on Cifar10. I recommend authors to use strong baselines, otherwise it is very difficult to convince the reader whether this method is useful in practice.\n\n- (2) Authors should share training curves and discuss whether why orthogonalization helps training dynamics or generalization (or both).\n\n- (4) Cost of motivated method needs to be discussed. Do we need to calculate SVD at every iteration. Can we start from previous bases? How does the cost and the gains compare with orthogonal initialization and regularization?\n\n- (5) Orthogonal basis is normalized, which affects the magnitude of the gradients;\ntherefore learning rate should be searched for SGD and proposed method separately using a validation set. If computational resources are scarce I recommend authors to focus on one of the resnets in cifar (instead of trying 5 different depths).\n\n## After Rebuttal\nI read authors response and most of the limitations (1,3,5) are still a concern, thus I keep my score and encourage authors to address them in the future revisions.\nNO.\n\nHowever, many of my concerns remain:\n\n* The performance of the baselines is still very low compared to number typically seen in the literature for similar experimental setups, which indicates that something is either missing from the presentation (to explain why they are so different), or that there is a technical problem with the experiments.",
            "test_text": "when using hyper-parameters tuned for SGDM, Orthogonal-SGDM underperforms SGDM (90.18% vs 91.17% on CIFAR-10), indicating that the gains are largely due to the method's robustness to poor hyper-parameter choices rather than a fundamental superiority over well-tuned baselines. Evidence anchors: SRC-007.\n\n- SIGN: The practical impact is limited by the computational overhead of the SVD operation, which the authors acknowledge is non-linear in matrix size and can be 'prohibitively expensive' for dense layers, without providing a detailed wall-clock time comparison to quantify the trade-off between iteration speed-up and per-step cost. Evidence anchors: SRC-011.\n\n- Suggested revision for SIGN opinion 4: Include a table comparing the total wall-clock time to reach a specific accuracy threshold for SGDM vs. Orthogonal-SGDM to demonstrate the net practical benefit.\n\n- ORIG: The experimental setup includes a diverse suite of models (BasicCNN, ResNets, DenseNets) on standard benchmarks (CIFAR-10, ImageNet) and a semi-supervised method (Barlow Twins), demonstrating the method's applicability beyond standard supervised classification and to different optimizers (LARS). Evidence anchors: SRC-008."
          },
          "QUAL-MET": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": 2,
              "alignment": 4,
              "average": 3.6666666666666665,
              "claim_extraction": "claim",
              "clarity": 5,
              "completeness": 3,
              "grounding_specificity": 5,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"claim\",\n  \"actionability\": 2,\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 5,\n  \"verifiability\": 3,\n  \"alignment\": 4,\n  \"completeness\": 3,\n  \"clarity\": 5\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": 3
            },
            "ref_text": "* The motivation for introducing this method is contradicted by the experimental results, so it is unclear why/when one might see benefits from using this approach.\nIn particular, the authors state that each randomly initialised unit in a layer will converge towards the same weights near the beginning of training. I do not really follow why this would be the case, and the experiments in Section 3 seem to directly contradict this.\n\n* It's good that the authors have removed the faulty motivation, but that only partially addresses my criticism: it's not obvious why orthogonalising the gradients is something we should do.",
            "test_text": "- QUAL: The mathematical formulation of the orthogonalization step is technically sound, correctly identifying the nearest orthonormal matrix via the SVD of the gradient matrix. Evidence anchors: SRC-004.\n\n- ORIG: The analysis in Section 3.1 contradicts the paper's central hypothesis, as the authors observe that Orthogonal-SGDM actually results in more similar component parameters (higher cosine similarity) than SGDM, which they describe as 'counter-intuitive' and leave for future study, undermining the theoretical justification. Evidence anchors: SRC-009."
          },
          "QUAL-REP": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": "X",
