              "completeness": 1,
              "grounding_specificity": 4,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"normal\",\n  \"actionability\": \"X\",\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": \"X\",\n  \"alignment\": 3,\n  \"completeness\": 1,\n  \"clarity\": 5\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": "X"
            },
            "ref_text": "Although the idea is quite straightforward, and there are a great many prior works on zero-shot and few-shot learning, the proposed technique is novel to my knowledge, and achieves state-of-the-art results on several  benchmark datasets. One addition that I think would improve the paper is a clearer description of the training algorithm (perhaps pseudocode). In its current form the paper a bit vague about this.",
            "test_text": "- CLAR: The paper is well-structured and clearly written, with a logical flow from introduction to method, experiments, and conclusion, and the core idea is explained concisely and intuitively. Evidence anchors: SRC-001, SRC-006."
          },
          "ORIG-MTH": {
            "judge_scores": {
              "actionability": 1,
              "alignment": 3,
              "average": 2.6666666666666665,
              "claim_extraction": "claim",
              "clarity": 4,
              "completeness": 2,
              "grounding_specificity": 4,
              "raw_response": "\n\n{\n  \"claim_extraction\": \"claim\",\n  \"actionability\": 1,\n  \"grounding_specificity\": 4,\n  \"verifiability\": 2,\n  \"alignment\": 3,\n  \"completeness\": 2,\n  \"clarity\": 4\n}",
              "success": true,
              "verifiability": 2
            },
            "ref_text": "The paper is an extension of the matching networks by Vinyals et al. in NIPS2016.\nInstead of using all the examples in the support set during test, the method represents each class by the mean of its learned embeddings. The training procedure and experimental setting are very similar to the original matching networks. I am not completely sure about its advantages over the original matching networks. It seems to me when dealing with 1-shot case, these two methods are identical since there is only one example seen in this class, so the mean of the embedding is the embedding itself.\nWhen dealing with 5-shot case, original matching networks compute the weighted average of all examples, but it is at most 5x cost. The experimental results reported for prototypical nets are only slightly better than matching networks. I  think it is a simple, straightforward,  novel extension, but I am not fully convinced its advantages.",
            "test_text": "- ORIG: The paper introduces a genuinely novel and streamlined algorithmic approach that replaces complex attention mechanisms with a simple mean-based prototype representation, offering a clear conceptual advance in simplicity and scalability. Evidence anchors: SRC-001, SRC-006."
          }
